chat {"x":-1422.2208251953125,"y":211.41119384765625,"width":600,"height":680,"madeBelow":116.87669372558594,"conversation":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","context":33805,"dollars":0.30955300000000002,"turns":[{"role":"user","text":"The Bi viz needs the cylinder to be positioned (which it is) AND oriented. I know the mathematics to calculate a quaternion for it, but I'm not sure how to supply it. I am pretty sure there's something built in just for this","message":0,"canvas":"sketchbook 3\naspect 1.5\nblock -653.453 -538.138 34 0 1 0 0 1000 0\n> \\text Vizes of Cl(3,0,1)\nend\nblock -653.453 -384.384 22 0 0 0 0 1000 0\n> \\text How the values of this algebra are drawn, on every canvas that works in it -- unless that canvas writes its own viz for the same type and window. Edit a box here and it is what every such canvas draws.\nend\nblock -768.768 -192.192 26 0 0 0 0 0 0\n> Cl(3,0,1);\n> Tri a;\n> Tri b;\n> Bi c = 0.1 * e12;\n> Tri a2 = -a;\n> \nval a.e012 -1.14541\nval a.e013 -1.216\nval a.e023 0.501578\nval a.e123 1\nval b.e012 1.74659\nval b.e013 -0.472873\nval b.e023 2.38321\nval b.e123 1\nend\nblock 115.315 153.754 12.0492 0 0 0 0 0 0\n> Viz(Tri, \"Space\");\n> // A point is a pair of cupped hands where the point is, mirrored when its\n> // weight (e123) is negative. A point at infinity has no position, only a\n> // direction: it is a ball out at the edge of everything -- bigger, since it\n> // is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\n> vec3 pointOf(Tri t) {\n>   vec3 d = vec3(t.e023, t.e013, t.e012);\n>   if (abs(t.e123) < 0.000001)\n>       return normalize(d + vec3(0.000001)) * 40.0;\n>   return d / t.e123;\n> }\n> void mesh() {\n>   for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) {\n>     float a0 = 3.14159 * float(i) / 12.0;\n>     float a1 = 3.14159 * float(i + 1) / 12.0;\n>     for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n>       float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n>       float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n>       vec3 p00 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b0), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b0));\n>       vec3 p01 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b1), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b1));\n>       vec3 p10 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b0), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b0));\n>       vec3 p11 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b1), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b1));\n>       emit(p00, p00);\n>       emit(p10, p10);\n>       emit(p11, p11);\n>       emit(p00, p00);\n>       emit(p11, p11);\n>       emit(p01, p01);\n>     }\n>   }\n> }\n> // The ball, only at infinity: anywhere else it folds away to nothing.\n> void vert(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n>   if (abs(v.e123) >= 0.000001) {\n>     p = pointOf(v);\n>     return;\n>   }\n>   sbUncut = true;\n>   p = pointOf(v) + p * 1.32;\n> }\n> // The hands, anywhere but infinity.\n> void mesh2() { model(\"cupped_hands_pair.obj\"); }\n> void vert2(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n>   if (abs(v.e123) < 0.000001) {\n>     p = pointOf(v);\n>     return;\n>   }\n>   if (v.e123 < 0.0) {\n>     p.x = -p.x;\n>     n.x = -n.x;\n>   }\n>   p = pointOf(v) + p * 0.3;\n> }\n> vec3 position(Tri v) {\n>   return pointOf(v);\n> }\n> float grab(Tri v) {\n>   return length(sbScreen(pointOf(v)) - sbPointer);\n> }\n> // Dragged in the plane through the point that faces the camera.\n> Tri drag(Tri v) {\n>   vec3 at = pointOf(v);\n>   vec3 facing = normalize(sbEye - at);\n>   float t = dot(at - sbRayOrigin, facing) / dot(sbRayDir, facing);\n>   vec3 to = sbRayOrigin + sbRayDir * t;\n>   return Tri(to.z, to.y, to.x, 1.0);\n> }\nend\nblock -615.014 192.192 14.3194 0 0 0 0 0 0\n> Viz(Bi,\"Space\");\n> \n> // A point is a lit ball where the point is. A point at infinity has no\n> // position, only a direction, so it goes out at the edge of everything --\n> // bigger, since it is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\n> \n> void mesh() {\n>   for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {\n>     float a0 = 20. * float(i-1);\n>     float a1 = 20. * float(i);\n>     for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n>       float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n>       float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n>       vec3 p00 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a0, sin(b0));\n>       vec3 p01 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a0, sin(b1));\n>       vec3 p10 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a1, sin(b0));\n>       vec3 p11 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a1, sin(b1));\n>       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n>       emit(p10, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n>       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n>       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n>       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n>       emit(p01, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n>     }\n>   }\n> }\n> \n> vec3 getPos(Bi v) {\n>   Tri retTri = (e123 | v) ^ (~v);\n>   return vec3(retTri.e023, retTri.e013, retTri.e012) / retTri.e123;\n> }\n> \n> vec3 position(Bi v) {\n>   return getPos(v);\n> }\n> \n> void vert(Bi v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n>   p = p + getPos(v);\n> }\n> \n> \n> \nend\nspace 0 1 0 0 2 3.14 -0.44 6.09275 1 1\npoint 2 a\npoint 2 b\npoint 2 c\npoint 2 a2\n"},{"role":"assistant","text":"I've updated b21 so the cylinder now lies along the line. I tested it in b1462, which draws $L = a \\vee b$ together with the points $a$ and $b$: the cylinder runs straight through both pairs of hands. Drag $a$ or $b$ in b19 and the cylinder turns to follow.\n\n**What's built in is the sandwich, `>>`.** No separate quaternion type or rotate-a-`vec3` helper exists. In Cl(3,0,1) a quaternion is just an `Even` that only uses `s, e12, e13, e23`: a rotor. To apply it to the mesh, b21 does this:\n\n- **Vertices:** each one becomes a `Tri` with weight 1, gets sandwiched, and is read back. That's the helper `turn`.\n- **Normals:** each normal gets the same treatment with weight 0, as a point at infinity. Translations don't move those, so only the rotation acts on a normal.\n\n**Making the rotor (`orient`).** Take $d$, the line moved to pass through the origin, and $y = e_{123} \\vee e_{013}$, the $y$ axis the mesh is built along. Both are normalised so they square to $-1$. Then\n$$R = \\frac{1 - d\\,y}{\\|1 - d\\,y\\|}$$\nis the rotor that turns half the angle between them, so $R\\,y\\,\\tilde R = d$. This is the standard rotor between two directions, and with the same normalisation it's a quaternion. One catch: if the line is nearly opposite to $y$, $1 - d\\,y$ shrinks towards zero. So past a quarter turn the code uses $1 + d\\,y$ instead, which aims the cylinder the other way. A cylinder looks the same either way round.\n\n**A shortcut you could use instead:** do the same thing with the full lines rather than the direction-only ones. $M = 1 - L\\,Y$, normalised (including its $e_{0123}$ part), is a **motor**, and on its own it moves and turns the $y$ axis onto $L$. I checked this numerically, and $M \\gg Y$ gives back $L$. The catch is that it puts the middle of the cylinder at a different point on the line, not at the point nearest the origin. That's why b21 still uses your `getPos` for the position.\n\n**Other changes in b21:**\n- The ring loop now runs `i < 2`. With `i < 3` the cylinder went from $-2.4$ to $4.8$, so it wasn't centred.\n- The header comment described the point viz, so I rewrote it to describe the line.\n- A line at infinity (one with no `e12 e13 e23` part) still divides by zero. It needs its own case, like the ball for points at infinity.\n\nThe cylinder looks short in the window. I think that's the walls cutting it, though I haven't checked. Setting `sbUncut` in `vert` would show the whole length."}],"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The canvas as it is now. Algebra: Cl(3,0,1).\n\nb17 (text box)\n  1| \\text Vizes of Cl(3,0,1)\n\nb18 (text box)\n  1| \\text How the values of this algebra are drawn, on every canvas that works in it -- unless that canvas writes its own viz for the same type and window. Edit a box here and it is what every such canvas draws.\n\nb19 (code box)\n  1| Cl(3,0,1);\n  2| Tri a;\n  3| Tri b;\n  4| Bi c = 0.1 * e12;\n  5| Tri a2 = -a;\n  6| \nCompiled.\nValues:\n  a : Tri (free: has a knob) = (e012 -1.145, e013 -1.216, e023 0.5016, e123 1)\n  b : Tri (free: has a knob) = (e012 1.747, e013 -0.4729, e023 2.383, e123 1)\n  c : Bi (derived) = (e01 0, e02 0, e12 0.1, e03 0, e13 0, e23 0)\n  a2 : Tri (derived) = (e012 1.145, e013 1.216, e023 -0.5016, e123 -1)\n\nb20 (viz box: Tri@Space)\n  1| Viz(Tri, \"Space\");\n  2| // A point is a pair of cupped hands where the point is, mirrored when its\n  3| // weight (e123) is negative. A point at infinity has no position, only a\n  4| // direction: it is a ball out at the edge of everything -- bigger, since it\n  5| // is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\n  6| vec3 pointOf(Tri t) {\n  7|   vec3 d = vec3(t.e023, t.e013, t.e012);\n  8|   if (abs(t.e123) < 0.000001)\n  9|       return normalize(d + vec3(0.000001)) * 40.0;\n 10|   return d / t.e123;\n 11| }\n 12| void mesh() {\n 13|   for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) {\n 14|     float a0 = 3.14159 * float(i) / 12.0;\n 15|     float a1 = 3.14159 * float(i + 1) / 12.0;\n 16|     for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n 17|       float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n 18|       float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n 19|       vec3 p00 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b0), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b0));\n 20|       vec3 p01 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b1), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b1));\n 21|       vec3 p10 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b0), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b0));\n 22|       vec3 p11 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b1), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b1));\n 23|       emit(p00, p00);\n 24|       emit(p10, p10);\n 25|       emit(p11, p11);\n 26|       emit(p00, p00);\n 27|       emit(p11, p11);\n 28|       emit(p01, p01);\n 29|     }\n 30|   }\n 31| }\n 32| // The ball, only at infinity: anywhere else it folds away to nothing.\n 33| void vert(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n 34|   if (abs(v.e123) >= 0.000001) {\n 35|     p = pointOf(v);\n 36|     return;\n 37|   }\n 38|   sbUncut = true;\n 39|   p = pointOf(v) + p * 1.32;\n 40| }\n 41| // The hands, anywhere but infinity.\n 42| void mesh2() { model(\"cupped_hands_pair.obj\"); }\n 43| void vert2(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n 44|   if (abs(v.e123) < 0.000001) {\n 45|     p = pointOf(v);\n 46|     return;\n 47|   }\n 48|   if (v.e123 < 0.0) {\n 49|     p.x = -p.x;\n 50|     n.x = -n.x;\n 51|   }\n 52|   p = pointOf(v) + p * 0.3;\n 53| }\n 54| vec3 position(Tri v) {\n 55|   return pointOf(v);\n 56| }\n 57| float grab(Tri v) {\n 58|   return length(sbScreen(pointOf(v)) - sbPointer);\n 59| }\n 60| // Dragged in the plane through the point that faces the camera.\n 61| Tri drag(Tri v) {\n 62|   vec3 at = pointOf(v);\n 63|   vec3 facing = normalize(sbEye - at);\n 64|   float t = dot(at - sbRayOrigin, facing) / dot(sbRayDir, facing);\n 65|   vec3 to = sbRayOrigin + sbRayDir * t;\n 66|   return Tri(to.z, to.y, to.x, 1.0);\n 67| }\nCompiled.\n\nb21 (viz box: Bi@Space)\n  1| Viz(Bi,\"Space\");\n  2| \n  3| // A point is a lit ball where the point is. A point at infinity has no\n  4| // position, only a direction, so it goes out at the edge of everything --\n  5| // bigger, since it is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\n  6| \n  7| void mesh() {\n  8|   for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {\n  9|     float a0 = 20. * float(i-1);\n 10|     float a1 = 20. * float(i);\n 11|     for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n 12|       float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n 13|       float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n 14|       vec3 p00 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a0, sin(b0));\n 15|       vec3 p01 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a0, sin(b1));\n 16|       vec3 p10 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a1, sin(b0));\n 17|       vec3 p11 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a1, sin(b1));\n 18|       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 19|       emit(p10, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 20|       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n 21|       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 22|       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n 23|       emit(p01, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n 24|     }\n 25|   }\n 26| }\n 27| \n 28| vec3 getPos(Bi v) {\n 29|   Tri retTri = (e123 | v) ^ (~v);\n 30|   return vec3(retTri.e023, retTri.e013, retTri.e012) / retTri.e123;\n 31| }\n 32| \n 33| vec3 position(Bi v) {\n 34|   return getPos(v);\n 35| }\n 36| \n 37| void vert(Bi v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n 38|   p = p + getPos(v);\n 39| }\n 40| \n 41| \n 42| \nCompiled.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"The Bi viz needs the cylinder to be positioned (which it is) AND oriented. I know the mathematics to calculate a quaternion for it, but I'm not sure how to supply it. I am pretty sure there's something built in just for this"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XoMa95E7RRCV56YgJjRPBv","name":"read_builtin_vizes","input":{},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XoMa95E7RRCV56YgJjRPBv","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Built-in vizes for types of no algebra (vizes.sketch):\n--- box 1\n\\text Vizes\n--- box 2\nViz(float, \"Axis\");\n// A real is a ring on the line, where the number is. The ring is chrome\n// rather than part of the scale, so it is measured in pixels of chrome --\n// sbPixel -- and stays one size however the line is zoomed.\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) {\n    float s = 6.28318 * float(i) / 24.0;\n    float e = 6.28318 * float(i + 1) / 24.0;\n    emit(vec3(0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n    emit(vec3(7.0 * cos(s), 7.0 * sin(s), 0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n    emit(vec3(7.0 * cos(e), 7.0 * sin(e), 0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n  }\n}\nvoid vert(float v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = vec3(v, 0.0, 0.0) + p * sbPixel;\n}\n// The hole in it, in the colour of the row.\nvoid mesh2() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) {\n    float s = 6.28318 * float(i) / 24.0;\n    float e = 6.28318 * float(i + 1) / 24.0;\n    emit(vec3(0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n    emit(vec3(3.0 * cos(s), 3.0 * sin(s), 0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n    emit(vec3(3.0 * cos(e), 3.0 * sin(e), 0.0), vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0));\n  }\n}\nvoid vert2(float v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = vec3(v, 0.0, 0.0) + p * sbPixel;\n}\nvec4 frag2(float v, vec3 p, vec3 n, vec2 uv) {\n  return vec4(28.0 / 255.0, 28.0 / 255.0, 32.0 / 255.0, 1.0);\n}\nvec3 position(float v) {\n  return vec3(v, 0.0, 0.0);\n}\n// Anywhere up or down the row counts: how far across is what matters.\nfloat grab(float v) {\n  return abs(sbScreen(position(v)).x - sbPointer.x);\n}\n// Wherever along the line the pointer is.\nfloat drag(float v) {\n  return sbRayOrigin.x;\n}\n--- box 3\nViz(RToR, \"Plot\");\n// A function of reals is a curve on a plot, input up and output across. A\n// pixel is on it by how near it is, measured square to the curve -- so a\n// steep stretch is as thick as a flat one.\nfloat gap(vec2 at) {\n  float h = (sbVertical.y - sbVertical.x) * 0.0025;\n  float slope = (value(at.y + h) - value(at.y - h)) / (2.0 * h);\n  return abs(at.x - value(at.y)) / sqrt(1.0 + slope * slope);\n}\n// By how much of the pixel the line covers, not in or out: in or out is a\n// staircase, and nothing but coverage smooths an edge a fragment decides.\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  float cover = clamp((sbThick - gap(at)) / max(fwidth(at.x), 0.000001) + 0.5, 0.0, 1.0);\n  return vec4(sbTint.rgb, sbTint.a * cover);\n}\nfloat grab(vec2 at) {\n  return gap(at) / sbPixel;\n}\n--- box 4\nViz(RToR, \"Axis\");\n// And a field along a number line: nothing at zero, opaque green at +1 and\n// opaque yellow at -1, clamped beyond -- so a colour means the same number\n// on every axis.\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  float v = clamp(value(at.x), -1.0, 1.0);\n  vec3 c = vec3(109.0, 205.0, 89.0) / 255.0;\n  if (v < 0.0) c = vec3(253.0, 231.0, 37.0) / 255.0;\n  return vec4(c, abs(v) * sbTint.a);\n}\n--- box 5\nViz(R2ToR3, \"Picture\");\n// A function of the plane is a picture: each pixel is the answer there, and\n// a vec3 is a colour.\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  return sbPaint(value(at));\n}\n--- box 6\nViz(R2ToC, \"Picture\");\n// A complex answer is domain colouring: hue for its argument, lightness for\n// its size.\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  return sbPaint(value(at));\n}\n--- box 7\nViz(CToC, \"Picture\");\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  return sbPaint(value(Complex(at.x, at.y)));\n}\n--- box 8\nViz(CToR3, \"Picture\");\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  return sbPaint(value(Complex(at.x, at.y)));\n}\n--- box 9\nViz(CToR, \"Picture\");\nvec4 frag(vec2 at) {\n  return sbPaint(value(Complex(at.x, at.y)));\n}\n--- box 10\nViz(R2ToR, \"Surface\");\n// A real function of the plane is a landscape, marched from wherever the\n// window has been turned to. sbMarch finds where a ray first meets it.\nvec4 shade(vec2 film, float pixel, out float nearest) {\n  vec3 o, d;\n  sbSurfaceCamera(film, o, d);\n  float t = sbMarch(o, d, pixel, nearest);\n  if (t < 0.0) return vec4(0.0);\n  vec3 hit = o + d * t;\n  return sbPaint(value(vec2(hit.x, hit.z)));\n}\n// Whether a ray hits is yes or no, so an edge is a staircase. A ray that\n// passed near an edge -- sbMarch says how near -- is marched four more times\n// across its pixel, and they are averaged by how much each covered.\nvec4 frag(vec2 film) {\n  vec2 px = vec2(fwidth(film.x), fwidth(film.y));\n  float pixel = px.x * sbHalfSpan();\n  float nearest;\n  vec4 c = shade(film, pixel, nearest);\n  if (nearest >= 1.5) return c;\n  vec4 sum = vec4(0.0);\n  float ignored;\n  for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {\n    vec2 j = vec2(0.375, 0.125);\n    if (k == 1) j = vec2(-0.125, 0.375);\n    if (k == 2) j = vec2(-0.375, -0.125);\n    if (k == 3) j = vec2(0.125, -0.375);\n    vec4 s = shade(film + j * px, pixel, ignored);\n    sum += vec4(s.rgb * s.a, s.a);\n  }\n  if (sum.a <= 0.0) return vec4(0.0);\n  return vec4(sum.rgb / sum.a, sum.a * 0.25);\n}\n--- box 11\nViz(R3ToR, \"Volume\");\n// A function of space is a cloud, looked through: thick where there is a lot\n// of it, clear where there is none, accumulated front to back so what is\n// nearer hides what is behind. sbClip is where the ray is inside the cube.\nvec4 frag(vec2 film) {\n  vec3 o, d;\n  sbVolumeCamera(film, o, d);\n  vec2 span = sbClip(o, d);\n  if (span.y <= span.x) return vec4(0.0);\n  float step = (span.y - span.x) / 96.0;\n  vec4 acc = vec4(0.0);\n  for (int i = 0; i < 96; ++i) {\n    float v = value(o + d * (span.x + (float(i) + 0.5) * step) - sbHub());\n    float a = 1.0 - exp(-sbThickness(v) * step * 6.0);\n    acc.rgb += (1.0 - acc.a) * sbColourOf(v) * a;\n    acc.a += (1.0 - acc.a) * a;\n    if (acc.a > 0.995) break;\n  }\n  if (acc.a <= 0.002) return vec4(0.0);\n  return vec4(acc.rgb / max(acc.a, 0.0001), acc.a);\n}\n--- box 12\nViz(R3ToC, \"Volume\");\n// The same cloud, coloured by argument and thickened by size.\nvec4 frag(vec2 film) {\n  vec3 o, d;\n  sbVolumeCamera(film, o, d);\n  vec2 span = sbClip(o, d);\n  if (span.y <= span.x) return vec4(0.0);\n  float step = (span.y - span.x) / 96.0;\n  vec4 acc = vec4(0.0);\n  for (int i = 0; i < 96; ++i) {\n    Complex v = value(o + d * (span.x + (float(i) + 0.5) * step) - sbHub());\n    float a = 1.0 - exp(-sbThickness(v) * step * 6.0);\n    acc.rgb += (1.0 - acc.a) * sbColourOf(v) * a;\n    acc.a += (1.0 - acc.a) * a;\n    if (acc.a > 0.995) break;\n  }\n  if (acc.a <= 0.002) return vec4(0.0);\n  return vec4(acc.rgb / max(acc.a, 0.0001), acc.a);\n}\n--- box 13\nViz(Color, \"Picker\");\n// A colour on the picker's ring, at its hue. The one the triangle points at is\n// also shown in the triangle, by the picker itself.\nvec3 where(Color c) {\n  float h = hsvOf(c).x * 6.28318;\n  return vec3(0.91 * cos(h), 0.91 * sin(h), 0.0);\n}\n// The colour it is, a disc; and outside a gap, a ring the colour of its\n// variable -- told apart by the texture coordinate, so the two are one piece\n// and a nearer marker is drawn whole over a farther one.\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) {\n    float s = 6.28318 * float(i) / 24.0;\n    float e = 6.28318 * float(i + 1) / 24.0;\n    vec3 a = vec3(cos(s), sin(s), 0.0);\n    vec3 b = vec3(cos(e), sin(e), 0.0);\n    vec3 n = vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);\n    emit(vec3(0.0), n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(b * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n  }\n}\n// Facing the viewer however the window is turned, one size on screen.\nvoid vert(Color v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = where(v) + (p.x * sbAcross() + p.y * sbUpward()) * sbPixel;\n}\nvec4 frag(Color v, vec3 p, vec3 n, vec2 uv) {\n  if (uv.x > 0.5) return sbTint;\n  return vec4(clamp(v, 0.0, 1.0), 1.0);\n}\nvec3 position(Color v) {\n  return where(v);\n}\nfloat grab(Color v) {\n  return length(sbScreen(where(v)) - sbPointer);\n}\n// Round the ring: the hue the pointer is at, the rest as it was.\nColor drag(Color c) {\n  vec3 k = hsvOf(c);\n  return hsv(fract(atan(sbRayOrigin.y, sbRayOrigin.x) / 6.28318), k.y, k.z);\n}\n--- box 14\nViz(Color, \"Cube\");\n// A colour in the RGB cube: red, green and blue along its three edges.\nvec3 where(Color c) {\n  return c - vec3(0.5);\n}\n// The colour it is, a disc; and outside a gap, a ring the colour of its\n// variable -- told apart by the texture coordinate, so the two are one piece\n// and a nearer marker is drawn whole over a farther one.\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) {\n    float s = 6.28318 * float(i) / 24.0;\n    float e = 6.28318 * float(i + 1) / 24.0;\n    vec3 a = vec3(cos(s), sin(s), 0.0);\n    vec3 b = vec3(cos(e), sin(e), 0.0);\n    vec3 n = vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);\n    emit(vec3(0.0), n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(b * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n  }\n}\n// Facing the viewer however the window is turned, one size on screen.\nvoid vert(Color v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = where(v) + (p.x * sbAcross() + p.y * sbUpward()) * sbPixel;\n}\nvec4 frag(Color v, vec3 p, vec3 n, vec2 uv) {\n  if (uv.x > 0.5) return sbTint;\n  return vec4(clamp(v, 0.0, 1.0), 1.0);\n}\nvec3 position(Color v) {\n  return where(v);\n}\nfloat grab(Color v) {\n  return length(sbScreen(where(v)) - sbPointer);\n}\n// In the plane through it that faces the viewer.\nColor drag(Color c) {\n  float t = dot(where(c) - sbRayOrigin, sbRayDir);\n  return sbRayOrigin + sbRayDir * t + vec3(0.5);\n}\n--- box 15\nViz(Color, \"Cylinder\");\n// A colour in the HSV cylinder: hue round it, saturation out from the middle,\n// value up.\nvec3 where(Color c) {\n  vec3 k = hsvOf(c);\n  float r = k.y * 0.5;\n  return vec3(r * cos(k.x * 6.28318), k.z - 0.5, r * sin(k.x * 6.28318));\n}\n// The colour it is, a disc; and outside a gap, a ring the colour of its\n// variable -- told apart by the texture coordinate, so the two are one piece\n// and a nearer marker is drawn whole over a farther one.\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 24; ++i) {\n    float s = 6.28318 * float(i) / 24.0;\n    float e = 6.28318 * float(i + 1) / 24.0;\n    vec3 a = vec3(cos(s), sin(s), 0.0);\n    vec3 b = vec3(cos(e), sin(e), 0.0);\n    vec3 n = vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);\n    emit(vec3(0.0), n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(b * 7.0, n, vec2(0.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 9.0, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(b * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n    emit(a * 11.5, n, vec2(1.0));\n  }\n}\n// Facing the viewer however the window is turned, one size on screen.\nvoid vert(Color v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = where(v) + (p.x * sbAcross() + p.y * sbUpward()) * sbPixel;\n}\nvec4 frag(Color v, vec3 p, vec3 n, vec2 uv) {\n  if (uv.x > 0.5) return sbTint;\n  return vec4(clamp(v, 0.0, 1.0), 1.0);\n}\nvec3 position(Color v) {\n  return where(v);\n}\nfloat grab(Color v) {\n  return length(sbScreen(where(v)) - sbPointer);\n}\n// In the plane through it that faces the viewer, read back as hue,\n// saturation and value.\nColor drag(Color c) {\n  vec3 q = sbRayOrigin + sbRayDir * dot(where(c) - sbRayOrigin, sbRayDir);\n  float h = fract(atan(q.z, q.x) / 6.28318);\n  return hsv(h, length(vec2(q.x, q.z)) / 0.5, q.y + 0.5);\n}\n\nBuilt-in vizes for this canvas's algebra (Cl(3,0,1) vizes.sketch):\n--- box 1\n\\text Vizes of Cl(3,0,1)\n--- box 2\n\\text How the values of this algebra are drawn, on every canvas that works in it -- unless that canvas writes its own viz for the same type and window. Edit a box here and it is what every such canvas draws.\n--- box 3\nCl(3,0,1);\nTri a;\nTri b;\nBi c = 0.1 * e12;\nTri a2 = -a;\n\n--- box 4\nViz(Tri, \"Space\");\n// A point is a pair of cupped hands where the point is, mirrored when its\n// weight (e123) is negative. A point at infinity has no position, only a\n// direction: it is a ball out at the edge of everything -- bigger, since it\n// is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\nvec3 pointOf(Tri t) {\n  vec3 d = vec3(t.e023, t.e013, t.e012);\n  if (abs(t.e123) < 0.000001)\n      return normalize(d + vec3(0.000001)) * 40.0;\n  return d / t.e123;\n}\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) {\n    float a0 = 3.14159 * float(i) / 12.0;\n    float a1 = 3.14159 * float(i + 1) / 12.0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n      float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n      float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n      vec3 p00 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b0), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b0));\n      vec3 p01 = vec3(sin(a0) * cos(b1), cos(a0), sin(a0) * sin(b1));\n      vec3 p10 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b0), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b0));\n      vec3 p11 = vec3(sin(a1) * cos(b1), cos(a1), sin(a1) * sin(b1));\n      emit(p00, p00);\n      emit(p10, p10);\n      emit(p11, p11);\n      emit(p00, p00);\n      emit(p11, p11);\n      emit(p01, p01);\n    }\n  }\n}\n// The ball, only at infinity: anywhere else it folds away to nothing.\nvoid vert(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  if (abs(v.e123) >= 0.000001) {\n    p = pointOf(v);\n    return;\n  }\n  sbUncut = true;\n  p = pointOf(v) + p * 1.32;\n}\n// The hands, anywhere but infinity.\nvoid mesh2() { model(\"cupped_hands_pair.obj\"); }\nvoid vert2(Tri v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  if (abs(v.e123) < 0.000001) {\n    p = pointOf(v);\n    return;\n  }\n  if (v.e123 < 0.0) {\n    p.x = -p.x;\n    n.x = -n.x;\n  }\n  p = pointOf(v) + p * 0.3;\n}\nvec3 position(Tri v) {\n  return pointOf(v);\n}\nfloat grab(Tri v) {\n  return length(sbScreen(pointOf(v)) - sbPointer);\n}\n// Dragged in the plane through the point that faces the camera.\nTri drag(Tri v) {\n  vec3 at = pointOf(v);\n  vec3 facing = normalize(sbEye - at);\n  float t = dot(at - sbRayOrigin, facing) / dot(sbRayDir, facing);\n  vec3 to = sbRayOrigin + sbRayDir * t;\n  return Tri(to.z, to.y, to.x, 1.0);\n}\n--- box 5\nViz(Bi,\"Space\");\n\n// A point is a lit ball where the point is. A point at infinity has no\n// position, only a direction, so it goes out at the edge of everything --\n// bigger, since it is far off, and exempt from the walls' cut.\n\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {\n    float a0 = 20. * float(i-1);\n    float a1 = 20. * float(i);\n    for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n      float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n      float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n      vec3 p00 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a0, sin(b0));\n      vec3 p01 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a0, sin(b1));\n      vec3 p10 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a1, sin(b0));\n      vec3 p11 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a1, sin(b1));\n      emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p10, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n      emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n      emit(p01, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvec3 getPos(Bi v) {\n  Tri retTri = (e123 | v) ^ (~v);\n  return vec3(retTri.e023, retTri.e013, retTri.e012) / retTri.e123;\n}\n\nvec3 position(Bi v) {\n  return getPos(v);\n}\n\nvoid vert(Bi v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  p = p + getPos(v);\n}\n\n\n\n"}]}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PwCCVqss2sQQ1PFZEsiDTg","name":"make_box","input":{"text":"Bi L = a & b;\nBi Y = e123 & e013;\nfloat nL = sqrt(L.e12 * L.e12 + L.e13 * L.e13 + L.e23 * L.e23);\nfloat nY = sqrt(Y.e12 * Y.e12 + Y.e13 * Y.e13 + Y.e23 * Y.e23);\nBi Ln = L / nL;\nBi Yn = Y / nY;\nEven M0 = 1.0 - Ln * Yn;\nEven MM = M0 * ~M0;\nEven M = M0 * (1.0 - MM.e0123 / (2.0 * MM.s) * e0123) / sqrt(MM.s);\nBi check = M >> Yn;\nTri o = M >> e123;\nTri up = M >> (e123 + e013);\nEven MMafter = M * ~M;"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PwCCVqss2sQQ1PFZEsiDTg","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Made b1462, beside the conversation.\nb1462 (code box)\n  1| Bi L = a & b;\n  2| Bi Y = e123 & e013;\n  3| float nL = sqrt(L.e12 * L.e12 + L.e13 * L.e13 + L.e23 * L.e23);\n  4| float nY = sqrt(Y.e12 * Y.e12 + Y.e13 * Y.e13 + Y.e23 * Y.e23);\n  5| Bi Ln = L / nL;\n  6| Bi Yn = Y / nY;\n  7| Even M0 = 1.0 - Ln * Yn;\n  8| Even MM = M0 * ~M0;\n  9| Even M = M0 * (1.0 - MM.e0123 / (2.0 * MM.s) * e0123) / sqrt(MM.s);\n 10| Bi check = M >> Yn;\n 11| Tri o = M >> e123;\n 12| Tri up = M >> (e123 + e013);\n 13| Even MMafter = M * ~M;\nCompiled.\nValues:\n  L : Bi (derived) = (e01 2.665, e02 -3.606, e12 -2.892, e03 -2.661, e13 -0.7431, e23 -1.882)\n  Y : Bi (derived) = (e01 0, e02 0, e12 0, e03 0, e13 -1, e23 0)\n  nL : float (derived) = 3.529\n  nY : float (derived) = 1\n  Ln : Bi (derived) = (e01 0.7552, e02 -1.022, e12 -0.8194, e03 -0.7539, e13 -0.2106, e23 -0.5331)\n  Yn : Bi (derived) = (e01 0, e02 0, e12 0, e03 0, e13 -1, e23 0)\n  M0 : Even (derived) = (s 1.211, e01 0.7539, e02 -0, e12 -0.5331, e03 0.7552, e13 -0, e23 0.8194, e0123 1.022)\n  MM : Even (derived) = (s 2.421, e01 5.922e-09, e02 -3.056e-08, e12 2.838e-08, e03 2.667e-08, e13 -5.182e-09, e23 1.729e-08, e0123 2.043)\n  M : Even (derived) = (s 0.778, e01 0.7067, e02 -0, e12 -0.3426, e03 0.3408, e13 -0, e23 0.5266, e0123 0.3283)\n  check : Bi (derived) = (e01 0.7552, e02 -1.022, e12 -0.8194, e03 -0.7539, e13 -0.2106, e23 -0.5331)\n  o : Tri (derived) = (e012 0.3053, e013 -0.8432, e023 1.445, e123 1)\n  up : Tri (derived) = (e012 1.125, e013 -0.6327, e023 1.979, e123 1)\n  MMafter : Even (derived) = (s 1, e01 1.194e-08, e02 -5.402e-09, e12 6.941e-09, e03 4.584e-09, e13 2.686e-09, e23 -8.056e-09, e0123 7.141e-08)\n"}]}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013rpc6AuZA6RnkJkaxDCxxn","name":"replace_box","input":{"box":"b21","text":"Viz(Bi,\"Space\");\n\n// A line is a long thin cylinder along it. The mesh is built along y, then\n// turned by a rotor R taking the y axis to the line's direction, and carried\n// to the line's point nearest the origin.\n\nvoid mesh() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {\n    float a0 = 20. * float(i-1);\n    float a1 = 20. * float(i);\n    for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n      float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n      float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n      vec3 p00 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a0, sin(b0));\n      vec3 p01 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a0, sin(b1));\n      vec3 p10 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a1, sin(b0));\n      vec3 p11 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a1, sin(b1));\n      emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p10, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n      emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n      emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n      emit(p01, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvec3 getPos(Bi v) {\n  Tri retTri = (e123 | v) ^ (~v);\n  return vec3(retTri.e023, retTri.e013, retTri.e012) / retTri.e123;\n}\n\n// The rotor (a quaternion: s, e12, e13, e23) turning the y axis onto the\n// line's direction: the line moved to the origin, times the y axis, plus one.\nEven orient(Bi v) {\n  float nv = sqrt(v.e12 * v.e12 + v.e13 * v.e13 + v.e23 * v.e23);\n  Bi d = (v.e12 * e12 + v.e13 * e13 + v.e23 * e23) / nv;\n  Bi y = e123 & e013;\n  Even R = 1.0 - d * y;\n  // More than a quarter turn: aim the other way (the cylinder doesn't mind),\n  // so R never shrinks to nothing.\n  if (R.s < 0.5) R = 1.0 + d * y;\n  Even RR = R * ~R;\n  return R / sqrt(RR.s);\n}\n\nvec3 turn(Even R, vec3 x, float w) {\n  Tri t = R >> Tri(x.z, x.y, x.x, w);\n  return vec3(t.e023, t.e013, t.e012);\n}\n\nvec3 position(Bi v) {\n  return getPos(v);\n}\n\nvoid vert(Bi v, inout vec3 p, inout vec3 n) {\n  Even R = orient(v);\n  p = turn(R, p, 1.0) + getPos(v);\n  n = turn(R, n, 0.0);\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_013rpc6AuZA6RnkJkaxDCxxn","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Changed b21.\nb21 (viz box: Bi@Space)\n  1| Viz(Bi,\"Space\");\n  2| \n  3| // A line is a long thin cylinder along it. The mesh is built along y, then\n  4| // turned by a rotor R taking the y axis to the line's direction, and carried\n  5| // to the line's point nearest the origin.\n  6| \n  7| void mesh() {\n  8|   for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {\n  9|     float a0 = 20. * float(i-1);\n 10|     float a1 = 20. * float(i);\n 11|     for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {\n 12|       float b0 = 6.28318 * float(j) / 16.0;\n 13|       float b1 = 6.28318 * float(j + 1) / 16.0;\n 14|       vec3 p00 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a0, sin(b0));\n 15|       vec3 p01 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a0, sin(b1));\n 16|       vec3 p10 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b0), a1, sin(b0));\n 17|       vec3 p11 = 0.12 * vec3(cos(b1), a1, sin(b1));\n 18|       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 19|       emit(p10, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 20|       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n 21|       emit(p00, vec3(cos(b0), 0., sin(b0)));\n 22|       emit(p11, vec3(cos(b1), 0., sin(b1)));\n 23|       emit(p01, vec3(cos(b1)