n(R, p, 1.0) + getPos(v);\n 59|   n = turn(R, n, 0.0);\n 60| }\n 61| \nCompiled.\n"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJ1F7bPhMMiVXZkpzYN5VN","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Changed b1462.\nb1462 (code box)\n  1| Bi L = a & b;\nCompiled.\nValues:\n  L : Bi (derived) = (e01 2.665, e02 -3.606, e12 -2.892, e03 -2.661, e13 -0.7431, e23 -1.882)\n"}]}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MtFUdzFDyEMxUQButPax18","name":"look","input":{"box":"b1462"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MtFUdzFDyEMxUQButPax18","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(A picture was here; look again to see it.)"},{"type":"text","text":"b1462 and its windows, as drawn now.\nb1462 (code box)\n  1| Bi L = a & b;\nCompiled.\nValues:\n  L : Bi (derived) = (e01 2.665, e02 -3.606, e12 -2.892, e03 -2.661, e13 -0.7431, e23 -1.882)\n"}]}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NQVf4WuppyEAD6pzA2GNk6","name":"replace_box","input":{"box":"b1462","text":"Bi L = a & b;\nTri pa = a;\nTri pb = b;"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PtiZpsdKLS6LobxtW13qiV","name":"look","input":{"box":"b1462"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NQVf4WuppyEAD6pzA2GNk6","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Changed b1462.\nb1462 (code box)\n  1| Bi L = a & b;\n  2| Tri pa = a;\n  3| Tri pb = b;\nCompiled.\nValues:\n  L : Bi (derived) = (e01 2.665, e02 -3.606, e12 -2.892, e03 -2.661, e13 -0.7431, e23 -1.882)\n  pa : Tri (derived) = (e012 -1.145, e013 -1.216, e023 0.5016, e123 1)\n  pb : Tri (derived) = (e012 1.747, e013 -0.4729, e023 2.383, e123 1)\n"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PtiZpsdKLS6LobxtW13qiV","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(A picture was here; look again to see it.)"},{"type":"text","text":"b1462 and its windows, as drawn now.\nb1462 (code box)\n  1| Bi L = a & b;\n  2| Tri pa = a;\n  3| Tri pb = b;\nCompiled.\nValues:\n  L : Bi (derived) = (e01 2.665, e02 -3.606, e12 -2.892, e03 -2.661, e13 -0.7431, e23 -1.882)\n  pa : Tri (derived) = (e012 -1.145, e013 -1.216, e023 0.5016, e123 1)\n  pb : Tri (derived) = (e012 1.747, e013 -0.4729, e023 2.383, e123 1)\n"}]}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I've updated b21 so the cylinder now lies along the line. I tested it in b1462, which draws $L = a \\vee b$ together with the points $a$ and $b$: the cylinder runs straight through both pairs of hands. Drag $a$ or $b$ in b19 and the cylinder turns to follow.\n\n**What's built in is the sandwich, `>>`.** No separate quaternion type or rotate-a-`vec3` helper exists. In Cl(3,0,1) a quaternion is just an `Even` that only uses `s, e12, e13, e23`: a rotor. To apply it to the mesh, b21 does this:\n\n- **Vertices:** each one becomes a `Tri` with weight 1, gets sandwiched, and is read back. That's the helper `turn`.\n- **Normals:** each normal gets the same treatment with weight 0, as a point at infinity. Translations don't move those, so only the rotation acts on a normal.\n\n**Making the rotor (`orient`).** Take $d$, the line moved to pass through the origin, and $y = e_{123} \\vee e_{013}$, the $y$ axis the mesh is built along. Both are normalised so they square to $-1$. Then\n$$R = \\frac{1 - d\\,y}{\\|1 - d\\,y\\|}$$\nis the rotor that turns half the angle between them, so $R\\,y\\,\\tilde R = d$. This is the standard rotor between two directions, and with the same normalisation it's a quaternion. One catch: if the line is nearly opposite to $y$, $1 - d\\,y$ shrinks towards zero. So past a quarter turn the code uses $1 + d\\,y$ instead, which aims the cylinder the other way. A cylinder looks the same either way round.\n\n**A shortcut you could use instead:** do the same thing with the full lines rather than the direction-only ones. $M = 1 - L\\,Y$, normalised (including its $e_{0123}$ part), is a **motor**, and on its own it moves and turns the $y$ axis onto $L$. I checked this numerically, and $M \\gg Y$ gives back $L$. The catch is that it puts the middle of the cylinder at a different point on the line, not at the point nearest the origin. That's why b21 still uses your `getPos` for the position.\n\n**Other changes in b21:**\n- The ring loop now runs `i < 2`. With `i < 3` the cylinder went from $-2.4$ to $4.8$, so it wasn't centred.\n- The header comment described the point viz, so I rewrote it to describe the line.\n- A line at infinity (one with no `e12 e13 e23` part) still divides by zero. It needs its own case, like the ball for points at infinity.\n\nThe cylinder looks short in the window. I think that's the walls cutting it, though I haven't checked. Setting `sbUncut` in `vert` would show the whole length."}]}]}}
